# --- Linear review categories ---------------------------------------------
#
# Linear reads these attributes to group the files in a pull request's diff
# ("File type" grouping) and to count implementation lines separately from
# tests and documentation. Linear counts implementation as "everything not
# claimed as something else", so a file that carries two categories is
# counted as implementation and the split stops working.
#
# Supported values:
#
#   review-implementation   the change itself
#   review-test             test code
#   review-documentation    prose and docs
#   review-generated        machine-generated or vendored output
#   review-agent-guidance   instructions for humans and AI agents
#   review-localization     translations (unused today; no locale trees)
#   review-assets           images and fonts
#
# WHY EVERY RULE IS THIS VERBOSE
#
# Git resolves each attribute name independently and the last matching rule
# wins per name. So negating a category only holds until some later rule sets
# it again -- negating inside a rule is not enough on its own. The invariant
# that actually holds is: the last matching rule sets one category and clears
# all six others, so it fully determines the file's state and every file lands
# in exactly one category. The rules below therefore look repetitive by design.
#
# Because each rule is self-contained, order only decides which category wins
# where patterns overlap, so the blocks run broad to specific and the most
# specific (tests) go last.
#
# Note `* text=auto` stays at the top of this file: it is the existing
# baseline that makes GitHub detect text and normalise line endings, and every
# rule here assumes it has already been applied.

# Baseline: everything is implementation until a rule below claims it.
* review-implementation

